<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1901–1929</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III was a Samoan paramount chief and leader of the Mau, the non-violent movement for self-government against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> rule. Imprisoned in New Zealand in 1928 for resisting taxes, he returned to Samoa in 1929. On 28 December 1929, Black Saturday, he was shot when New Zealand police fired on a peaceful Mau procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, and he died the next day, urging his followers to keep the peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii.jpg" alt="Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred John Tattersall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mau_leaders_and_Tupua_Tamasese_Lealofi_III_in_front_of_Mau_office_1929.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
